virglrenderer: Venus inlines the calls of modules whose functions take an array by value

What: vkCreateShaderModule on MoltenVK (KosmicKrisp compiles such modules), after the scan
and spirv-val passed the guest's code: when a function of the module takes an array by value
(an OpFunctionParameter of an OpTypeArray type, and the module calls something), its calls
are inlined into its entry points
(spirv-opt --merge-return --inline-entry-points-exhaustive --eliminate-dead-functions, for
the device's SPIR-V target, ids capped at VKR_SPIRV_MAX_BOUND; new src/venus/
vkr_spirv_inline.c). The result is scanned and validated as strictly as guest code (always
enforced, also in a log-only build) and must have no array parameter left; then the driver
gets it, the module keeps its scan (the pipeline checks see what the driver compiles) and
the module is charged for the code the drivers copy. Before the optimizer runs: the guest's
code must pass spirv-val strictly (a log-only build lets invalid code on, and the optimizer
reads what spirv-val took only; the validator's cache answers), and the inlined size,
estimated from the call graph in one linear pass (each function's words plus its callees'
inlined size once per call), must be under VKR_SPIRV_INLINE_MAX_BYTES (1 MB): a module whose
functions call each other twice, 20 levels deep, would else have the inliner make 2^20
copies on the ring thread. After it: an entry point the guest's code kept inside the
Function and Private element cap (default sizes) must stay inside it (inlined, a callee's
variables are one copy per call), or the pipeline check would refuse a module that worked
before. If any of that fails, the guest's code goes to the driver as it is and the app's
context goes on; the 1st, 2nd, 4th, 8th ... such module says why (virgl_warn). A successful inline is one vkr_log line (info). Modules without an array
parameter are not touched (4 of the corpus' 180-odd: Zed's vs_quad and
fs_path_rasterization, SPIR-V 1.4 and 1.6). Shader code chained into a pipeline stage
(VK_KHR_maintenance5) is not inlined.
Why: Zed 1.27.0 (gpui's wgpu renderer) ended at its first frame on MoltenVK. MoltenVK
1.4.2's SPIRV-Cross declares a by-value array parameter as spvUnsafeArray<T, 2> but passes
a buffer struct's member as the plain T[2] it declares there, and Metal refuses the call:
  program_source:374:16: error: no matching function for call to '_350'
  note: candidate function not viable: no known conversion from '_13[2]' to
  'spvUnsafeArray<_13, 2>' for 4th argument
so vkCreateGraphicsPipelines failed (VK_ERROR_INITIALIZATION_FAILED, "Vertex shader
function could not be compiled into pipeline"), the guest, which makes pipelines without
waiting, bound the handle, vkr had no object for it ("failed to look up object 134 of type
19", "vkCmdBindPipeline resulted in CS error") and the context was lost; the guest's Venus
driver aborted in vn_EndCommandBuffer. naga writes helper functions as functions and passes
arrays by value; inlined, there is no call for SPIRV-Cross to get wrong.
Upstream: no; the SPIRV-Cross bug is not reported yet (Zed's modules are in
Tests/virgl/spirv-corpus/pass/naga-zed-*).
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-venus-spirv-validate.sh (inline unit on the CPU: exactly Zed's
vs_quad and fs_path_rasterization modules are inlined, pass the scan and spirv-val for both
drivers' targets and have no array parameter, and no other corpus module is touched; a module
whose calls double 24 levels deep is refused by the size estimate, quickly, without the
optimizer); test-venus-commands.c
p-gfx-naga-array-call-ok on the staged MoltenVK (Zed's quad and path rasterization
pipelines are made; before this patch both failed with -3).

